WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.110
Bird Box made a quick nest for itself in the
pop culture zeitgeist, and viewers are wondering

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.960
if they'll see any more from that world.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.680
Netflix hasn't announced any plans to produce
a sequel to its record-breaking hit, but that

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.000
doesn't mean we won't see a follow-up at some
point.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.960
Here's everything we know about a possible
Bird Box sequel.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.320
Netflix proudly touted Bird Box as an instant
success, proclaiming that it had been watched

00:00:22.329 --> 00:00:26.980
by more than 45 million accounts in its first
week of release — the best opening ever

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.120
for the streaming service.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.079
Netflix Chief Content Officer Ted Sarandos
previously compared the 20 million streams

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.000
for The Christmas Chronicles to a $200 million
movie opening.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.520
By that metric, Bird Box netting more than
double those views would easily make it one

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.320
of the biggest movies of 2018.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.380
The accuracy of the analogy can be debated,
but even if Netflix is exaggerating the numbers,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.760
the movie still seems like a staggering success.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.180
And in Hollywood, success usually brings sequels.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.900
Such was the case with Bright, the fantasy
buddy-cop movie starring Will Smith and Joel

00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.400
Edgerton.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.860
After a strong opening on the platform with
11 million reported streams in its first three

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.680
days, Netflix made the decision to go ahead
with a sequel.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.780
It's also a movie that had a far larger production
budget than Bird Box — $90 million compared

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.560
to $19.8 million.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.720
For the moment, Netflix has said that there
are no plans to make Bird Box 2, but that

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.100
doesn't mean the idea has been totally ruled
out.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.080
After fans clamored for a follow-up to the
teen rom-com To All the Boys I've Loved Before,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.640
the company announced a second film.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.680
Of course, the situation is a little different
with that movie, which is based on the first

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.360
book of a trilogy by Jenny Han.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:36.800
In other words, there's source material out
there for a second To All The Boys feature.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.460
That's not the case with Bird Box.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.600
"The only thing that will happen soon, is
we're all gonna die soon."

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.400
Bird Box is based on the Josh Malerman novel
of the same name, though screenwriter Eric

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.220
Heisserer and director Susanne Bier did make
some changes to suit the film.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.880
Most of the characters in the movie either
aren't in the book, or have been altered from

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.230
the book, but the most notable change to how
the story actually feels is the ending, which

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.800
Heisserer purposely altered to make more hopeful.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.960
But despite these changes, neither version
of the story wraps up with an obvious sequel

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:07.460
hook.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.020
One approach producers could take is to look
at another Malerman novel, Black Mad Wheel,

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.870
for a similar glimpse into a world where people's
senses start betraying them.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.110
In that book, a group of musicians is sent
to an African desert to determine the source

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.820
of a sound that's wreaking havoc on the population.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:27.000
Perhaps this phenomenon could be tied in with
the monsters from Bird Box, which cause insanity

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:27.500
on sight.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.400
Of course, there's no rule saying that there
has to be source material to continue a franchise.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.920
Game of Thrones has famously surpassed George
R.R. Martin's original Song of Ice and Fire

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.840
novels in its final seasons, with the author
giving the show's producers permission to

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.440
guide the story how they see fit.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.360
Netflix could try for a similar arrangement
with Malerman.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.070
The point is, they don't need source material
to keep the story spinning — especially

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:52.100
considering how much the movie already deviated
from the book.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:57.420
Not long after Bird Box was crowned a success,
People magazine asked Susanne Bier if she'd

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:58.950
be interested in directing a sequel.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.360
Bier was non-committal, saying, quote, "we
only just finished it," encouraging fans to

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.400
thoroughly enjoy the first movie before clamoring
for another.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.240
But she was also clearly happy with the movie's
reception, and that kind of popular acclaim

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:15.040
might be just the thing to inspire the filmmaker
to explore a second go-around.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.320
It's easy to see why a project like Bird Box
would attract a talent like Bier.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:23.410
The award-winning director has a long résumé
of films that focus on families in times of

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.160
crisis, most notably 2010's Oscar-winning
In a Better World.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.120
Bird Box features plenty of the same themes,
but with a sci-fi twist.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.340
Bier also seems to be interested in exploring
themes of motherhood.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:39.870
In late 2018, she signed on to direct HBO's
limited series The Undoing, in which Big Little

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.170
Lies star Nicole Kidman plays a woman left
to rebuild a life with her young son after

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.900
a series of devastating revelations about
her husband.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.650
With that kind of work under her belt, there's
little surprise that Bier would be attracted

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.900
to telling the story of Malorie, raising the
kids she never wanted.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.500
It was also the director's first attempt at
a horror film.

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:00.780
While she has no sequels in her filmography
now, a follow-up to Bird Box could involve

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.180
a deeper exploration of the themes the first
movie introduced, possibly being enough to

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.340
interest Bier and her collaborators in another
trip down the river.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.680
It was a classic end-of-the-world love story.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.460
Boy meets girl.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:13.760
Girl hates boy.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:17.960
Before too long, boy and girl end up getting
to know one another on top of a washing machine.

00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:20.810
It could be the start of something beautiful
— but how does it end?

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:24.680
The last time we saw Felix and Lucy, they
were sneaking into the garage to steal the

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.840
recently-deceased Greg's car and leaving the
rest of the housemates stranded.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.840
When asked about it by People, Susanne Bier
left the couple's destination up to the imagination,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:34.340
saying

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.740
"I actually think it's quite exciting that
we don't know exactly where they went."

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.539
Rosa Salazar, who played Lucy in the movie,
has her own idea of what happens next for

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.040
the duo.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.840
She told the Los Angeles Times that she imagines
the pair surviving and realizing that leaving

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.060
the house was the best possible outcome.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.720
Salazar described her sequel as a combination
of True Romance and Fear and Loathing in Las

00:04:54.729 --> 00:04:58.639
Vegas, with both characters evading death
thanks to their indulging in the temporary

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.180
insanity of drugs and alcohol.

00:05:00.409 --> 00:05:03.880
The idea of the two young lovers speeding
down the highway like a psychedelic Bonnie

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.380
and Clyde has some appeal to it.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:08.560
Yet it begs the question of what happens when
they run out of gas?

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.920
Or worse yet… out of drugs?

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.839
If the film makers decide to take the franchise
in an edgier direction, they might want to

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.100
give Salazar a call.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.819
Bird Box has drawn comparisons to A Quiet
Place, with both movies being thrillers about

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:25.159
families trying to evade monsters that kill
by taking advantage of one of the senses.

00:05:25.159 --> 00:05:29.309
Unlike A Quiet Place, Bird Box never shows
the creatures, but that wasn't necessarily

00:05:29.309 --> 00:05:30.020
by design.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.600
Sandra Bullock admitted that she filmed a
scene with one of the creatures, but it was

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:37.529
eventually scrapped — probably for the best,
since the original design for the monsters

00:05:37.529 --> 00:05:38.660
mostly made her laugh.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.140
That didn't stop fans from wondering what
the monsters looked like.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.971
In place of actually seeing a visualization
of the monster, the final version of the movie

00:05:45.971 --> 00:05:51.270
has a scene in which the monster-allied Gary
sketches a series of disturbing images, supposedly

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.530
based on what the monsters looked like to
him.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:57.300
It was good enough for one movie, but there's
really only so long that you can leave the

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.820
bad guys off-screen while maintaining suspense.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.180
Any sequel would have to give audiences at
least a glimpse of what's causing all the

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:04.680
horror.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.000
If the filmmakers decide to stick with the
current look, they'll have to figure out a

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.520
way to make the creatures a lot less laughable.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:18.400
"...making the end of the world great again!

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.420
Yooooooo."

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.400
We know what the Bird Box monsters do to people,
but we don't know much beyond that.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.740
In the movie, Lil Rel Howery's character Charlie
has his own theory on what the monsters are,

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:35.380
likening them to the demons that live in the
mythology of every civilization's spiritual

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:35.920
traditions.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.260
"All different names for the same thing."

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:40.140
"And what's that?"

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:43.260
"The end of us."

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.280
In real life, the credit for that goes to
Heisserer, who came up with the idea on his

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:48.780
own.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:52.960
In Malerman's original book, the author never
gave his bad guys a backstory.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.539
Instead, the screenwriter dug into various
legends and folklore to create a plausible

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.860
origin for the monsters.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:02.399
It's another way in which Bird Box differs
from A Quiet Place.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.120
John Krasinski says he created an entire history
for his monsters in order to better understand

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:07.620
them.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.819
Bird Box, on the other hand, isn't really
concerned with the nature of the hostile invaders,

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:16.069
though Heisserer has admitted that he drew
some inspiration from the mysterious aliens

00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:18.200
in his 2016 movie Arrival.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.660
That still doesn't fully answer the question
of whether Bird Box's monsters are aliens,

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.820
spirits, or something even stranger.

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:29.160
Did these creatures arrive on Earth from somewhere
else, or have they always been here?

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.220
If so, what woke them up?

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:36.319
The characters speculate that before you go
crazy and commit suicide, you see images of

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.480
something from your past that's believed to
involve a deep sense of loss or intense sadness.

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:46.160
Lydia wonders if she sees her dead parents
just moments before taking a seat behind the

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.900
steering wheel of a burning car.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:53.300
Malorie was unsuccessfully tempted by Tom's
disembodied voice to take off her blindfold,

00:07:53.309 --> 00:07:57.509
and we're still wondering what made the tracksuit-wearing
woman in the hospital want to start smashing

00:07:57.509 --> 00:07:59.249
her head into a glass window.

00:07:59.249 --> 00:08:03.809
We may never find out what the brain-scrambling
final images really are, because very few

00:08:03.809 --> 00:08:05.700
people are left alive to talk about it.

00:08:05.929 --> 00:08:09.919
The ones that do live through their encounters
with the monsters don't seem all that reliable,

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.640
turning into homicidal maniacs wanting to
share their ideas of something "beautiful."

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:18.939
Considering the varied responses, it's hard
to imagine that everyone's seeing the same

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.920
thing — one person's beauty could be another
person's nightmare.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:26.260
We're still trying to figure out what could
be so beautiful as to inspire a new mother

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.920
to take a running header out of a second-floor
window.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:33.840
Americans like to think of themselves as a
can-do bunch, which could explain how a plucky

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:38.340
bunch from Northern California figured out
how to evade certain death from an unseen

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:38.840
menace.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.780
But the movie establishes that the arrival
of the monsters is a spreading, global phenomenon,

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:47.240
suggesting that there may be many other groups
around the world who have figured out some

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.860
way to stay alive in the face of a mass calamity.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:53.820
When the movie opens, we learn that the panic
has hit Europe and is spreading quickly.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.540
But we also never see the solutions other
people around the world come up with.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.380
Since the film has already taken liberties
with the source material, it wouldn't be a

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.211
stretch for the filmmakers to examine the
panic from another part of the world to see

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.260
how diverse groups of people are coping.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.320
If a sequel wanted to stay a little closer
to home, a potential storyline could follow

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:13.880
the residents of the Janet Tucker School for
the Blind.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:18.190
While the students weren't in any danger of
being driven to suicide, they almost certainly

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.380
came into contact with people who were afflicted.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:24.600
How long did it take them to understand what
was happening and why they weren't stricken

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:25.320
with madness?

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.650
It would also be interesting to discover how
the school became a haven for refugees seeking

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:30.200
shelter.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.240
Now that Malorie and the kids have made it
to the safety of the compound, they're all

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.600
ready to start new lives.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:38.480
Yet we have no clue what those lives will
be like.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:42.350
From all appearances, the people sheltered
inside the School for the Blind are leading

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.680
happy, productive lives, but it's hard to
believe that it will be peace and harmony

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:46.180
forever.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.500
More people are likely to come looking for
help.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.740
There's also the chance that the deranged
will come looking to create chaos.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:56.460
And there are still unseen horrors looking
to do damage outside the walls.

00:09:56.589 --> 00:10:01.560
A potential sequel could see Tom and Olympia
becoming acclimated to their new society,

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.920
and also show the school training its residents
to survive in the outside world.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:09.420
If the people inside gain enough knowledge
of what the creatures actually are, they could

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.860
come up with a plan to eventually defeat them.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:16.180
Or the kids could simply choose to live out
their days in tranquility, building relationships

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.660
with the people they meet in their new living
situation.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.520
It'd be a happy ending, even if it might not
be the most exciting sequel.

